1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:06,440
Teachers shape students' futures — but why does their judgment get distorted at the most delicate moment?

2
00:00:06,440 --> 00:00:13,759
In Italy, the end-of-middle-school guidance advice heavily determines who will reach university and who will not.

3
00:00:13,759 --> 00:00:17,620
Take two students, both with an average of eight in mathematics,

4
00:00:17,620 --> 00:00:20,920
one wealthy, the other economically disadvantaged.

5
00:00:20,920 --> 00:00:25,300
Once they enter the teacher's decision filter, their destinies diverge.

6
00:00:25,300 --> 00:00:28,500
The well-off student is steered toward a scientific liceo.

7
00:00:28,500 --> 00:00:31,579
The disadvantaged one is directed to a technical school.

8
00:00:31,579 --> 00:00:34,179
Despite identical grades.

9
00:00:34,179 --> 00:00:37,420
The reason is a structural blind spot.

10
00:00:37,420 --> 00:00:42,620
Teachers almost never get real feedback on the later careers of their former students.

11
00:00:42,620 --> 00:00:48,659
Working in the dark, they massively overestimate the risk that poorer students will fail.

12
00:00:48,659 --> 00:00:55,460
The fix: give teachers a personalized report with the true promotion rates.

13
00:00:55,460 --> 00:01:00,899
Reading objective data on their past choices, the distorted perception of risk collapses.

14
00:01:00,899 --> 00:01:03,620
The filter recalibrates immediately.

15
00:01:03,620 --> 00:01:08,819
The system stops penalizing income and redirects talent toward academic high schools.

16
00:01:08,819 --> 00:01:11,819
And that bright, disadvantaged student from the beginning?

17
00:01:11,819 --> 00:01:15,420
Now enters a liceo and passes the first year without trouble.

18
00:01:15,420 --> 00:01:18,669
Objective data has just wiped out an involuntary bias.
